WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.087
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.087 --> 00:00:05.688
Building Provider Notices.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:09.500
This is Chapter 7: the
weekly digest, and evidence.

4
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:14.062
The digest is for the people who act;
the evidence is for the people who check.

5
00:00:14.062 --> 00:00:18.337
Both come from the same runs, so neither
can drift from what was actually read.

6
00:00:18.337 --> 00:00:23.419
We'll start from what the implementation
guide asks the security officer to keep,

7
00:00:23.419 --> 00:00:26.984
then read the digest's five
sections, the evidence record,

8
00:00:26.984 --> 00:00:31.687
the chain and the stage check, and walk
two labs, the evidence and the dangers.

9
00:00:31.687 --> 00:00:33.263
Two notes before we begin.

10
00:00:33.263 --> 00:00:36.625
This chapter is a draft,
awaiting the owner's review.

11
00:00:36.625 --> 00:00:40.500
And the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

12
00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.350
Why put the digest and the
evidence in one chapter?

13
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:50.875
Because they serve two different
audiences from the same runs.

14
00:00:50.875 --> 00:00:53.813
The digest is for the
people who act on notices.

15
00:00:53.813 --> 00:00:56.938
The evidence is for the people
who check that the work happened.

16
00:00:56.938 --> 00:00:59.161
If both come from the same run folders,

17
00:00:59.161 --> 00:01:02.100
neither can drift from what
the kit actually read.

18
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:06.819
By the end of this chapter you can write
the weekly digest from a collection and the

19
00:01:06.819 --> 00:01:10.775
routing state, record every run as
evidence the security officer can

20
00:01:10.775 --> 00:01:15.150
verify with a hash, and say which of
a stage's steps have a passing record.

21
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:23.762
Start from what the implementation guide's
evidence section asks for, gate by gate.

22
00:01:23.762 --> 00:01:29.719
From each provider, Azure, Microsoft
365, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure,

23
00:01:29.719 --> 00:01:35.162
AWS and Google Cloud, the raw
output, the notices and the manifest.

24
00:01:35.162 --> 00:01:38.974
From routing, the payloads, the
routing table and the state.

25
00:01:38.974 --> 00:01:43.024
And the digests, preflight
results and evidence records,

26
00:01:43.024 --> 00:01:45.350
with an index and a file of hashes.

27
00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:49.732
The guide maps them to NIST controls
for external services, advisories,

28
00:01:49.732 --> 00:01:53.148
change control, contingency
planning, risk assessment,

29
00:01:53.148 --> 00:01:57.975
monitoring and audit protection, and to
the indicators FedRAMP lists for them.

30
00:01:57.975 --> 00:02:02.450
The kit's records support those
controls; the assessor decides them.

31
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.100
Naming the evidence isn't
the same as having it.

32
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.558
Owners read a page, not a JSON file,

33
00:02:11.558 --> 00:02:16.050
so the kit writes the digest as a
readable page and a spreadsheet.

34
00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:20.487
A folder of files can be changed
after the fact, so each run is checked

35
00:02:20.487 --> 00:02:24.687
against its manifest, copied, and
recorded with every file's hash.

36
00:02:24.687 --> 00:02:29.863
Someone has to say which run supports which
control, so each record names its steps.

37
00:02:29.863 --> 00:02:33.087
A record removed from the
middle would go unnoticed,

38
00:02:33.087 --> 00:02:36.087
so each record holds the
hash of the one before it.

39
00:02:36.087 --> 00:02:40.390
And a go/no-go needs to know
which steps still lack evidence.

40
00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:47.398
The digest reads a collection
and the routing state,

41
00:02:47.398 --> 00:02:50.278
and writes a page, a
spreadsheet and a manifest.

42
00:02:50.278 --> 00:02:52.141
The page has five sections.

43
00:02:52.141 --> 00:02:57.228
Open notices by provider, counted by
kind and by whether action is required.

44
00:02:57.228 --> 00:02:59.531
Action required, soonest due first,

45
00:02:59.531 --> 00:03:02.789
each with the assignment
group the routing named.

46
00:03:02.789 --> 00:03:06.127
Maintenance and changes starting
in the next thirty days.

47
00:03:06.127 --> 00:03:09.653
What's new in the last seven days,
by when the kit first saw it.

48
00:03:09.653 --> 00:03:13.665
And what closed, or stopped being
listed, in the last seven days.

49
00:03:13.665 --> 00:03:18.160
The two windows are parameters, set
in the scheduled run's settings.

50
00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:23.372
Now the evidence record.

51
00:03:23.372 --> 00:03:25.947
Recording a run folder does four things.

52
00:03:25.947 --> 00:03:30.023
It re-hashes every file the
run's own manifest lists.

53
00:03:30.023 --> 00:03:32.672
It copies the folder
into the evidence store.

54
00:03:32.672 --> 00:03:37.938
It writes a record with the time, the
environment, the mode, the change record, the

55
00:03:37.938 --> 00:03:43.122
command, and the result, which is a pass
only if every file matches its manifest.

56
00:03:43.122 --> 00:03:47.641
The record names the steps it covers,
with their controls and indicators,

57
00:03:47.641 --> 00:03:52.085
the claim language, every file's hash,
and the hash of the record before it.

58
00:03:52.085 --> 00:03:54.735
Then it appends the
index and rewrites a sums

59
00:03:54.735 --> 00:03:57.750
file that a standard
checksum tool can check.

60
00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.763
Each kind of run covers named steps.

61
00:04:03.763 --> 00:04:08.975
A collection covers one collect step for
each provider it read, plus normalizing.

62
00:04:08.975 --> 00:04:10.795
A routing run covers routing,

63
00:04:10.795 --> 00:04:14.587
and the contingency step if a
payload asked for a plan review.

64
00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:16.987
A digest covers the digest step.

65
00:04:16.987 --> 00:04:19.799
The runbook in Chapter 8 adds pre-flight,

66
00:04:19.799 --> 00:04:23.037
the scheduled run, the
alert plan and rollback.

67
00:04:23.037 --> 00:04:26.187
And every record covers
the evidence step itself.

68
00:04:26.187 --> 00:04:29.394
Two things make the folder
checkable: the sums file,

69
00:04:29.394 --> 00:04:32.524
which says every file still
matches, and the chain,

70
00:04:32.524 --> 00:04:37.920
where each record holds the previous record's
hash, so a removed record breaks it.

71
00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:42.820
Then there's the check.

72
00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:48.033
On its own, it verifies the sums file
against every file and walks the chain.

73
00:04:48.033 --> 00:04:51.970
With a stage, it adds a row
for each step that stage needs.

74
00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:55.266
A test stage needs
pre-flight, the scheduled run,

75
00:04:55.266 --> 00:04:59.070
routing and the digest;
production adds the alert plan.

76
00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:01.490
Any failing row exits with an error,

77
00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:04.995
which is exactly what the
runbook's go or no-go reads.

78
00:05:04.995 --> 00:05:08.278
The tests cover the digest's
sections and counts,

79
00:05:08.278 --> 00:05:13.695
the record's fields and claim language, the
chain, a changed file, a removed record,

80
00:05:13.695 --> 00:05:18.980
a failed run, a stage's missing steps,
and the requirements catalog itself.

81
00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:23.780
Who's involved?

82
00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:27.605
The owners read the digest each
week and act on their rows.

83
00:05:27.605 --> 00:05:32.023
The security officer owns this
gate: files each week's digest,

84
00:05:32.023 --> 00:05:35.380
keeps the records, and runs
the go or no-go check.

85
00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:39.117
Developers maintain the digest,
the record and the chain.

86
00:05:39.117 --> 00:05:41.705
One caution matters here
more than anywhere.

87
00:05:41.705 --> 00:05:45.317
The evidence chain protects
files from undetected change.

88
00:05:45.317 --> 00:05:47.005
It doesn't decide a control.

89
00:05:47.005 --> 00:05:49.851
Keep the evidence folder
on agency storage,

90
00:05:49.851 --> 00:05:52.980
with the access the security officer sets.

91
00:05:56.730 --> 00:06:00.842
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7 with
Pester, in the kit's folder.

92
00:06:00.842 --> 00:06:03.095
Step 1 makes a week of runs:

93
00:06:03.095 --> 00:06:07.817
Chapter 6's two collections
and two routings, in one go.

94
00:06:07.817 --> 00:06:10.038
Day one routes twelve creates;

95
00:06:10.038 --> 00:06:14.392
the second week makes one create,
four updates and one close.

96
00:06:14.392 --> 00:06:17.280
Step 2 writes the digest
for the second week.

97
00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:22.970
Sixteen open notices, eight needing action,
five starting in the next thirty days,

98
00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:25.942
two new, and four closed
or no longer listed.

99
00:06:25.942 --> 00:06:29.600
It writes the page, the
spreadsheet and a manifest.

100
00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:36.613
Step 3 reads the digest
as the owners will.

101
00:06:36.613 --> 00:06:42.718
The first table counts open notices by
provider and kind: Azure, Microsoft 365,

102
00:06:42.718 --> 00:06:45.389
Oracle Cloud Infrastructure, AWS,

103
00:06:45.389 --> 00:06:49.300
and Google Cloud, which
has nothing open this week.

104
00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:52.563
Then the action-required
table, soonest due first,

105
00:06:52.563 --> 00:06:54.950
each with the team the routing named.

106
00:06:54.950 --> 00:06:59.339
An Oracle Cloud certificate
bundle to replace by December 15,

107
00:06:59.339 --> 00:07:05.106
a Microsoft 365 retirement of basic
authentication for SMTP client submission,

108
00:07:05.106 --> 00:07:07.688
the Azure retirement from Chapter 1.

109
00:07:07.688 --> 00:07:12.000
And at the bottom, the two with
no date, shown as not stated.

110
00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:18.475
The rest of the digest
looks ahead and back.

111
00:07:18.475 --> 00:07:23.461
Maintenance and changes starting in the
next thirty days: an AWS database window,

112
00:07:23.461 --> 00:07:25.396
two Azure maintenance windows,

113
00:07:25.396 --> 00:07:29.563
and the rescheduled Oracle Cloud
database maintenance in Ashburn.

114
00:07:29.563 --> 00:07:35.475
New in the last seven days: an Azure
maintenance and a Microsoft 365 change.

115
00:07:35.475 --> 00:07:40.943
And closed or no longer listed: the AWS
security notification and other notices

116
00:07:40.943 --> 00:07:46.163
the providers closed, plus the Azure
incident that's simply no longer listed.

117
00:07:46.163 --> 00:07:49.348
The last line says the
digest is neutral by design:

118
00:07:49.348 --> 00:07:53.000
what providers published and
the routing the agency set.

119
00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:55.290
File it with the run's evidence.

120
00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:01.712
Step 4 records three runs as evidence:

121
00:08:01.712 --> 00:08:05.678
the second week's collection,
its routing, and the digest.

122
00:08:05.678 --> 00:08:09.262
Each prints its record number,
a pass, the steps it covers,

123
00:08:09.262 --> 00:08:12.691
and the controls and indicators
those steps support.

124
00:08:12.691 --> 00:08:16.515
The collection covers all five
providers and normalizing.

125
00:08:16.515 --> 00:08:20.590
The digest covers the digest
step, and adds risk assessment.

126
00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:25.368
Notice the routing run: it adds the
contingency step, and contingency planning

127
00:08:25.368 --> 00:08:30.800
among its controls, because one of its
payloads asked for a contingency plan review.

128
00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:38.847
Step 5 checks the chain:
twenty-five files match the sums,

129
00:08:38.847 --> 00:08:41.462
and three records, chain unbroken.

130
00:08:41.462 --> 00:08:46.426
Step 6 checks the test stage's coverage,
and here a failure is the right answer.

131
00:08:46.426 --> 00:08:50.933
A lab has no pre-flight and no
scheduled run, so those two steps fail,

132
00:08:50.933 --> 00:08:55.275
routing and the digest pass, and
the command exits with an error.

133
00:08:55.275 --> 00:08:59.713
That's exactly what a go or no-go
would see: the gap, by name.

134
00:08:59.713 --> 00:09:03.872
Step 7 reads the records'
index: three records, collect,

135
00:09:03.872 --> 00:09:08.300
route and digest, each a pass,
with the steps it covers.

136
00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:14.287
Step 8 is the one to remember.

137
00:09:14.287 --> 00:09:15.782
Change a recorded file,

138
00:09:15.782 --> 00:09:19.713
by appending one line to the
recorded digest, and check again.

139
00:09:19.713 --> 00:09:20.876
Both checks fail.

140
00:09:20.876 --> 00:09:22.817
The sums say the digest changed,

141
00:09:22.817 --> 00:09:26.850
and the chain says it no longer
matches its record, naming the file.

142
00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:30.075
That's what protects the evidence
from an edit after the fact.

143
00:09:30.075 --> 00:09:35.588
Step 9 runs the kit's whole test suite:
a hundred and forty passed, none failed.

144
00:09:35.588 --> 00:09:38.260
Before step 8, you had
an evidence folder whose

145
00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:41.074
sums file you could hand
to the security officer.

146
00:09:41.074 --> 00:09:43.640
Delete the output folder when you're done.

147
00:09:47.390 --> 00:09:53.152
Lab 2 uses real run folders from any earlier
chapter's second lab, in a test tenant,

148
00:09:53.152 --> 00:09:58.128
account, tenancy or project, never
production, kept on agency storage.

149
00:09:58.128 --> 00:10:02.965
Route and digest the test collection with
the lab's routing and a new state file.

150
00:10:02.965 --> 00:10:05.843
Record all three runs,
this time with the test

151
00:10:05.843 --> 00:10:08.565
change record's number,
and check the chain.

152
00:10:08.565 --> 00:10:11.587
Then give the digest to the
test environment's owners,

153
00:10:11.587 --> 00:10:15.365
and ask whether each action-required
row reached the right person.

154
00:10:15.365 --> 00:10:19.224
To back it out, keep the stage's
evidence with its change record,

155
00:10:19.224 --> 00:10:22.750
and delete only the copies
outside agency storage.

156
00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:30.312
Here's what this gate proves,
against NIST's control catalog.

157
00:10:30.312 --> 00:10:34.620
The digest shows the owners got the
week's notices, what needs action,

158
00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:38.775
by when and who owns it, which
supports disseminating advisories,

159
00:10:38.775 --> 00:10:42.237
monitoring external services,
and responding to risk.

160
00:10:42.237 --> 00:10:44.656
Each record, with its
hashes and the chain,

161
00:10:44.656 --> 00:10:48.712
supports continuous monitoring
and protecting audit information.

162
00:10:48.712 --> 00:10:53.150
A folder that's unchanged, and a change
that's caught, is audit protection.

163
00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:56.149
And a stage check that
names every gap supports

164
00:10:56.149 --> 00:10:58.687
continuous monitoring at the go or no-go.

165
00:10:58.687 --> 00:11:01.987
The indicators are FedRAMP's,
mapped by the program.

166
00:11:01.987 --> 00:11:06.160
The records support these controls;
the assessor decides them.

167
00:11:09.910 --> 00:11:11.310
What breaks in production?

168
00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:17.672
The quietest failure is the weekly run
stopping: no digest, no evidence, no tasks.

169
00:11:17.672 --> 00:11:19.703
The chain shows the gap in dates,

170
00:11:19.703 --> 00:11:23.297
and the go or no-go fails
without a passing scheduled run.

171
00:11:23.297 --> 00:11:26.348
A recorded file edited
later is caught by the sums

172
00:11:26.348 --> 00:11:29.473
and the record's hashes, and
the check names the file.

173
00:11:29.473 --> 00:11:32.248
A record removed from the
middle breaks the chain.

174
00:11:32.248 --> 00:11:35.892
A digest sent outside the
agency publishes which clouds

175
00:11:35.892 --> 00:11:39.072
and services matter, so it
stays on agency storage.

176
00:11:39.072 --> 00:11:43.900
And a pass is not a control decision:
each record's claim says so.

177
00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:49.000
Here's what done looks like.

178
00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:53.075
The digest reaches every owner
the routing names, every week.

179
00:11:53.075 --> 00:11:56.361
Every collection, routing
run and digest is recorded,

180
00:11:56.361 --> 00:11:58.900
with the change record where there is one.

181
00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:01.137
The check passes on the evidence folder.

182
00:12:01.137 --> 00:12:06.249
The stage check lists every step the go
or no-go needs, and each one passes.

183
00:12:06.249 --> 00:12:09.345
And the evidence folder
lives on agency storage,

184
00:12:09.345 --> 00:12:11.975
with the access the security officer set.

185
00:12:11.975 --> 00:12:17.600
Next, Chapter 8 runs all of it, first in
a test environment and then in production.

186
00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:18.750
Thanks for watching.
